.TH ERF 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
erf, erff, erfl, erfc, erfcf, erfcl \- error and complementary error functions
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double erf(double " x );
.br
.BI "float erff(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double erfl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double erfc(double " x );
.br
.BI "float erfcf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double erfcl(long double " x );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR erf ()
function returns the error function of
.IR x ,
defined mathematically as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
            2     /x       2
erf(x) = ------ * |  exp(-t ) dt
         sqrt(pi) /0
.EE
.RE
.PP
.BR erf ()
is an odd function \(em
.BI erf(\- x ") = \-erf(" x )
\(em with domain the entire real line and range
.BR [\-1,\ +1] .
It satisfies
.B erf(0) = 0,
.B erf(+\[if]) = +1,
and
.BR "erf(\-\[if]) = \-1" .
.PP
The
.BR erfc ()
function returns the complementary error function of
.IR x ,
defined as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
erfc(x) = 1 \- erf(x)
.EE
.RE
.PP
The range of
.BR erfc ()
is
.BR [0,\ 2] ,
with
.B erfc(0) = 1,
.B erfc(+\[if]) = 0,
and
.BR "erfc(\-\[if]) = 2" .
.BR erfc ()
exists as a separate function because, for large positive
.IR x ,
.I erf(x)
approaches 1 and the naive expression
.I "1.0 \- erf(x)"
loses all significant digits to catastrophic cancellation;
.BR erfc ()
should always be preferred when the quantity of interest is the small
tail probability rather than the bulk integral.
.PP
The
.BR erff ()
and
.BR erfcf ()
variants take and return
.B float
values; the
.BR erfl ()
and
.BR erfcl ()
variants take and return
.B "long double"
values.
.PP
.SS Substrate Implementation
The Substrate libm implements
.BR erf ()
using the Abramowitz & Stegun 7.1.26 rational approximation, with a
maximum absolute error of approximately
.BR 1.5e\-7 .
This is significantly less accurate than the IEEE\ 754
correctly-rounded result and is sufficient only for moderate-precision
use; it will not pass an
.B "ulp \[<=] 1"
conformance test against a reference implementation.  For arguments
with magnitude greater than 6.0 the result is clamped to
.BR \(+-1 ,
which is exact to double precision because the true value differs from
.B \(+-1
by less than
.BR 2^\-53 .
.PP
.BR erfc ()
in the current revision is implemented as
.B "1.0 \- erf(x)"
with explicit special-value handling for
.B \(+-\[if]
and NaN.  This inherits the precision of
.BR erf ()
and additionally suffers cancellation for large positive
.IR x ,
where it should be replaced by an asymptotic expansion in a future
revision; callers requiring accurate small-tail values for
.I "x \[>=] 4"
should treat the result as indicative only.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR erf ()
returns the error function of
.I x
in the closed interval
.BR [\-1,\ +1] ,
and
.BR erfc ()
returns the complementary error function of
.I x
in the closed interval
.BR [0,\ +2] .
.PP
If
.I x
is +0 (\-0),
.BR erf ()
returns +0 (\-0); the sign of zero is preserved because
.BR erf ()
is odd.
.PP
If
.I x
is +0 or \-0,
.BR erfc ()
returns +1.
.PP
If
.I x
is positive infinity,
.BR erf ()
returns +1 and
.BR erfc ()
returns +0.  If
.I x
is negative infinity,
.BR erf ()
returns \-1 and
.BR erfc ()
returns +2.
.PP
If
.I x
is a NaN, both functions return a NaN.
.PP
Neither function ever produces an overflow, underflow, or domain error
for any finite or infinite argument: the mathematical range of
.BR erf ()
is bounded by 1 and that of
.BR erfc ()
is bounded by 2, both of which are exactly representable in every
supported floating-point format.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
No errors are defined for
.BR erf ()
or
.BR erfc ().
They never set
.I errno
and never raise a floating-point exception, even when the argument is
a NaN, an infinity, or a signed zero.  In particular,
.B FE_INVALID
is not raised for a NaN argument because NaN propagation is the only
defined behaviour, and
.B FE_OVERFLOW
cannot occur because the mathematical range of both functions is
bounded.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR erf (),
.BR erff (),
.BR erfl (),
.BR erfc (),
.BR erfcf (),
.BR erfcl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR erf (),
.BR erfc (),
and their
.BR f / l
variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The handling of special
arguments follows C99 Annex F.10.5 (IEC 60559).  The accuracy of the
result is implementation-defined; the Substrate implementation
explicitly does not meet IEEE\ 754 correct rounding (see
.B "Substrate Implementation"
above).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program computes
.B erf(1.0)
and
.B erfc(1.0)
and verifies the identity
.BI erf( x ") + erfc(" x ") = 1"
to the accuracy of the implementation:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double x = 1.0;
    double e  = erf(x);
    double ec = erfc(x);

    printf("erf(%.1f)  = %.10f\en", x, e);   /* ~ 0.8427007929 */
    printf("erfc(%.1f) = %.10f\en", x, ec);  /* ~ 0.1572992071 */
    printf("erf + erfc = %.10f\en", e + ec); /* ~ 1.0          */

    /* erf is odd: erf(-x) == -erf(x). */
    printf("erf(\-%.1f) = %.10f\en", x, erf(\-x));

    /* Special values. */
    printf("erf(0)        = %g\en",  erf(0.0));
    printf("erfc(0)       = %g\en",  erfc(0.0));
    printf("erf(+INF)     = %g\en",  erf(INFINITY));
    printf("erfc(+INF)    = %g\en",  erfc(INFINITY));
    printf("erfc(\-INF)    = %g\en", erfc(\-INFINITY));
    printf("isnan(erf(NAN)) = %d\en", isnan(erf(NAN)));

    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A representative output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
erf(1.0)  = 0.8427007929
erfc(1.0) = 0.1572992071
erf + erfc = 1.0000000000
erf(\-1.0) = \-0.8427007929
erf(0)        = 0
erfc(0)       = 1
erf(+INF)     = 1
erfc(+INF)    = 0
erfc(\-INF)    = 2
isnan(erf(NAN)) = 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
The least-significant digits of
.B erf(1.0)
may differ from a reference value by up to
.B 1.5e\-7
on Substrate; the identity
.BI erf( x ") + erfc(" x ") = 1"
holds exactly only because
.BR erfc ()
is currently computed as
.IR "1.0 \- erf(x)" .
